From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/a733 de: write CCSC_CTL = 0, measured on silicon

``drm-a733-display-engine-dram-into-tcon-tv1`` enabled the channel colour-space converter and wrote the unity matrix,
and left the question open: is `ctl = 1` with a unity matrix right (what the
vendor does), or `ctl = 0` (the block disabled entirely, which a linear-RGB
layer needs no coefficients for)? Both were made reachable from the debugfs
`ccsc` verb so one board session could settle it. That session ran on the 8 GiB board on
2026-09-19 and the answer is 0, on two independent silicon readings.

1. The reset value is 0, not 1. `state` captures `cold_ccsc` before this driver
   writes anything; on a cold DE it reads `0x00000000`. The argument in the
   header -- eight instances reading 1 on the vendor's live link make 1 the
   reset default -- is false. The 1 is the vendor's own configuration, which it
   writes together with 24 coefficient words.

2. Both settings put the same pixels on the wire. The sink's own frame CRC is
   the only observer downstream of this block, and it is identical for `ctl = 1`
   and `ctl = 0` across five flat fields -- the three primaries, white and
   mid-grey -- with two captures each:

       pattern      ccsc   crc R/G/B
       0x00ff0000   1      0x132f/0x0000/0x0000
       0x00ff0000   0      0x132f/0x0000/0x0000
       0x0000ff00   1      0x0000/0x132f/0x0000
       0x0000ff00   0      0x0000/0x132f/0x0000
       0x000000ff   1      0x0000/0x0000/0x132f
       0x000000ff   0      0x0000/0x0000/0x132f
       0x00ffffff   1      0x132f/0x132f/0x132f
       0x00ffffff   0      0x132f/0x132f/0x132f
       0x00808080   1      0xa11a/0xa11a/0xa11a
       0x00808080   0      0xa11a/0xa11a/0xa11a

   `0x00020000` is unity, now confirmed on the wire rather than inferred from a
   diagonal. The same table proves there is no channel swap in this path: red
   moves only the R CRC, green only G, blue only B.

So the block is transparent either way, and the choice is not a hardware fact.
It is decided on cost: `ctl = 0` needs no coefficient words, and 24 words whose
encoding this part's UM does not document anywhere are 24 chances to be wrong.
That the A523/T527 series also writes 0 is a coincidence of outcome and not a
reason -- another part is a hint, never a verification target, and it settled
nothing here.

Both values stay reachable from `ccsc`, because the measurement above is only
repeatable while they are.

Measured with a script on the board.
This closes a previously open question about CTL.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-de.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-de.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-de.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-de.c
@@ -293,17 +293,27 @@
  * framebuffer really does contain red; and no clock change can help, because
  * the pixels are being multiplied by zero.
  *
- * ⚠ AND IT MEANS THE ARGUMENT FOR WRITING 1 WAS WRONG. This driver reasoned
- * that eight instances reading 1 on the vendor's link made 1 the reset default.
- * The vendor also writes 24 coefficient words, so it CONFIGURES this block
- * rather than leaving it alone -- which is evidence that 1 is the vendor's
- * value, not the reset. The A523/T527 series writes ctl = 0 for a linear-RGB
- * layer, and that reading is now the more likely of the two.
- *
- * SO BOTH ARE REACHABLE FROM debugfs `ccsc`, and the board settles it in one
- * session instead of two: 1 (default) writes the vendor's configuration --
- * enabled with a unity matrix -- and 0 disables the block the way the prior art
- * does. HYPOTHESIS, not proof: nothing here has been on a board.
+ * MEASURED ON the 8 GiB board, 2026-09-19, AND THE ANSWER IS 0. Two independent readings,
+ * both from silicon:
+ *
+ *   1. The reset value is 0, not 1. `state` captures cold_ccsc before this
+ *      driver writes anything, and on a cold DE it reads 0x00000000. The old
+ *      argument here -- eight instances reading 1 on the vendor's link make 1
+ *      the reset default -- is therefore false: 1 is the vendor's own
+ *      configuration, which it writes together with 24 coefficient words.
+ *
+ *   2. Both settings put the SAME pixels on the wire. The sink's own frame CRC,
+ *      the only observer downstream of this block, is identical for ctl = 1 and
+ *      ctl = 0 across five flat fields -- the three primaries, white and
+ *      mid-grey (five patterns x two
+ *      settings x two captures). 0x00020000 is unity, confirmed.
+ *
+ * So the block is transparent either way and the choice is not a hardware fact.
+ * It is decided on what the two configurations cost: ctl = 0 needs no
+ * coefficient words at all, and 24 words whose encoding this part's UM does not
+ * document are 24 chances to be wrong. That the A523/T527 series also writes 0
+ * is a coincidence of outcome, not a reason -- another part is a hint, never a
+ * verification target, and it was not what settled this.
  */
 #define  CCSC_COEFF_ROW0		0x0010
 #define  CCSC_COEFF_ROW_STRIDE		0x0010
@@ -876,15 +886,14 @@ static void sun60i_de_ccsc_one(struct sun60i_de *de, u32 base)
  * written, and printed by `state`.
  *
  * CCSC (channel colour-space conversion), slot + 0x800, eight instances at a
- * 0x100 stride. Live: every one of the eight reads 0x00000001, on a board where
- * exactly ONE layer of ONE channel was fetching -- which is the argument that 1
- * is this block's reset default rather than something the vendor configured.
- * The A523 series writes 0 here for a linear-RGB layer; that is code, on a
- * different part, and our own silicon says 1 while producing correct colour on
- * this part. Silicon beats code, so this driver writes 1.
- *   FALSIFIED BY: wrong colour -- channels swapped, or washed out -- which
- *   would mean the block is on the path with non-unity coefficients and the
- *   answer is 0. One line, and cold_ccsc in `state` says which way to go.
+ * 0x100 stride. Live on the VENDOR's link every one of the eight reads
+ * 0x00000001, and that was once read as the reset default. cold_ccsc settled it
+ * the other way: on a cold DE the same register reads 0x00000000, so the 1 is
+ * the vendor's configuration. This driver writes 0, and the sink's frame CRC
+ * says the wire cannot tell the difference (2026-09-19, the header above).
+ *   FALSIFIED BY: a picture that is black, or wrong in colour, with ctl = 0 --
+ *   which would mean the block is not a bypass when disabled. One write of the
+ *   `ccsc` verb tries the other reading without a rebuild.
  *
  * VSU (the per-channel scaler), slot + 0x4000. Live: VSU_CTL and
  * ATTR_OUT_SIZE both read zero and there is a picture, so a 1:1 unscaled path
@@ -1631,7 +1640,7 @@ static int sun60i_de_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	de->dev = dev;
 	de->mode = sun60i_display_mode_1920x1200;
 	de->background = BLD_COLOR_BLACK;
-	de->ccsc_on = true;
+	de->ccsc_on = false;
 	de->commit = &sun60i_de_commit_mmio;
 	platform_set_drvdata(pdev, de);
 
